WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.660
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.660
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:21.420
 In this fifth season, we're speaking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists about migrations of all kinds.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:31.860
 We'll hear about food and the experience of leaving home and in finding new ones, of decolonizing food traditions and tracing recipes through the movements of diaspora.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.870
 Delicious Revolution is made by Devon Samson and Chelsea Wills.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:40.920
 You can subscribe to the show on Apple Podcasts or wherever you get your podcasts.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:47.920
 And you can find all of our episodes, along with pictures and more, on the website deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:55.560
 Ayhan Aydin is a meal ecologist.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.560
 His interdisciplinary practice consists of projects that consider art, science, and food.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.850
 He is interested in creating stories through meals and challenging the idea of what a meal is.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:12.320
 As a cook, he likes to take ordinary food items and create something unexpected but still familiar.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.320
 He's one of the founders of Nordic Food Development, a company that creates new food products from Nordic Ingredients,

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.320
 applying various culinary traditions to existing foods in order to utilize and repurpose them.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:38.780
 His artistic work includes collaborations with open restaurant, a theater play called The Restaurant Rite of Commons, featuring a meal as a central part of the story, and several conceptual dinners.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:52.160
 In this episode, Ayhan talked with Devon about making tempeh from Swedish vava beans, what it means to create something new from very old food traditions, and drawing on the richness of many immigrant food traditions.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.160
 Let's see.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:02:02.440
 So, tell me about Nordic Food Development, and what is it?

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.470
 It's my small company I have with a friend.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:17.920
 The idea was when I was going to school, to university, when I was studying meal ecology, and just at the end of the education, I just came up with it.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.410
 I did some things at school.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.810
 I did some deep fried split peas.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:24.810
 I made a sauce.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:31.700
 I did a bunch of things that people really liked, and the split peas was like a hit.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:37.250
 So, I was thinking, and I was like, shouldn't I start to produce food?

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.090
 I can do products.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.600
 I can produce stuff before.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:43.570
 Right.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:52.690
 In the same way as, you know, DIY culture, like people did their small record labels or whatnot.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:57.370
 I was thinking that I could do the same, but with food.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:02.340
 You can have this small independent indie-like food companies.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.550
 It's hard though, but it's hard to have everything that's independent.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.720
 Oh, right.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.130
 So, yeah.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:17.260
 I was just thinking, going this kind of DIY hardcore culture where I come from, and just do it into food.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.390
 So, we had this idea with split peas.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.450
 We didn't have all the money.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.570
 Then, I made a sauce.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.130
 Never thought of that sauce as an awesome sauce.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.480
 Then, people started to try it.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:36.770
 We had it for a project for a theater play in the summer of 2013.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:41.170
 There's a free theater thing in Stockholm.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.170
 It's a city theater, whereas in the parks.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:54.730
 And one of the projects this year was with an artist duo who made a project about the commons we have here in Sweden in the nature.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:04:03.250
 So, we used that sauce and from there, people kind of liked it and were like, yeah, we should do.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:07.790
 We start with this because it's very easy and simple and see where it is.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:14.580
 And it's been a way to experiment on how to create a food product.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:21.950
 So, because my angle on this is that to use, where we are at.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:27.880
 Like, we live in Sweden, try to use so much things that grows here.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:35.530
 But to use culture and knowledge from elsewhere and combine them in some ways.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:42.240
 And some things, the sauce is very much just a mashup of two existing products.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:50.050
 So, that was like very much of how I did music for like 20 years sampling.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:53.050
 I just took things and mashed them together, added some stuff.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.020
 Then I had a track.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.560
 So, we did the song in the same way.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:08.520
 We took soy sauce, then we took distilled vinegar and added some things in the vinegar and just mashed them together, made the sauce.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.740
 I come from, my food culture is really strong for my family and my mom's a chef.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:18.190
 And in Turkey, people eat lots of nuts and they use chickpeas a lot.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:22.370
 And the split peas in Sweden is very kind of similar to it.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:31.280
 So, I was, yeah, why can't I do a similar thing as people do in Turkey or Middle East with the split peas?

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:38.870
 So, I just took the split peas, boiled them, in a way the industry boils, because the split peas easily break.

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:43.440
 But I found a way that people do in the industry, but I could do it myself.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.300
 So, we just cooked them and boiled them.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.260
 Then we cooked them and we deep fried them.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.960
 And we came up with this awesome snack.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:59.260
 And then I started to have contact with producers and tried a certain pea.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:06.050
 And that was like super great, but we didn't have the money then.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.050
 So, that project is kind of on hold.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:17.670
 But that is very much of me taking the culture, my personal culture, my family's history and whatnot.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:32.490
 And taking like ideas and knowledge from science and the industry and see what we have in Sweden, produce wise, and try to figure out to do a product.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:36.580
 And the same thing has been with a recent project I had with a farmer.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.580
 We did tempeh from her fava beans.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:45.100
 And the fava beans is, the ones they use for feed is not often eaten here.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:48.190
 And in all the world, people eat lots of fava beans.

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:57.460
 And my friend Erika, she was told from other farmers that you can't eat these peas, these beans.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.720
 And I was like, yes, you can.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:06.870
 People just eat lots of in Egypt or lots of places in the world.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:11.670
 So, and it's easy to produce here in Sweden.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.810
 It has high protein, very delicious.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:23.150
 And the one they use for feed is more complex in its sweet notes and has really awesome nuttiness.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:28.940
 It's very similar to, it's very chestnut-y in a way.

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:32.350
 So we just came up with the idea to take tempeh.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:34.110
 Tempeh is so easy to make.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:40.860
 It's an easy protein product that you can basically do yourself.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.370
 Then we took these beans.

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:51.160
 And in Sweden right now, it's a huge discussion about turning to eat more plant-based proteins.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:08:01.590
 And what we eat, because she comes from craft and small, she's not small scale, but for a smaller farmer.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:08.860
 And then I'm coming from trying to mix both craft and industry thinking.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:19.610
 We were like, yeah, but lots of the proteins we eat today, that's so plant-based, they're very from the industry and have industry taste no.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:23.810
 They don't have deliciousness as a primary idea.

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:28.910
 And our idea was to make a delicious protein product.

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:34.290
 And tempeh has been going on for decades, for centuries.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:36.320
 And lots of people try it, like it.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:44.700
 And the ones that we made is quite different from the other peas people usually do, because they're much denser.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:50.460
 And this is more fluffy, more and kind of spacious in a way.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:54.740
 It's very fluffy, because the way the beans are, they're porous.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:59.940
 And when you do the tempeh, it becomes more porous.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:07.080
 And it's not that dense, but it can easily be added, like, paste through fat and stuff.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.270
 Right.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:09.270
 Yeah, wow.

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:11.270
 Man, I got to taste it sometime.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:12.740
 Yeah.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:19.800
 Because I kind of always liked tempeh, people don't eat it here, and started to introduce even to my friends who are vegans.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:23.310
 And they were like, "Oh, shit, this is good."

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:28.460
 And lots of friends who are not vegans, or vegetarians, who just like deliciousness.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:34.030
 One guy was like, "This is the greatest thing I ate in 2016."

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:34.960
 Wow, wow.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:42.850
 And he is really good at food, and he works with a food company, and he was like, "This is good."

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:56.990
 And I think, often, that's been my kind of idea with Nordic food development, to take old ideas that's good, and just keep going with them, and just try to develop them.

00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:59.460
 Because you can always develop food.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:05.900
 Food is always constant development, or at least food culture is.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:12.940
 Because every time, if you're not doing industry-wise, there's always going to be a small change in it, whatever you do.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:19.800
 You can be a super skilled chef, or you're always doing small adjustments.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:25.920
 It's going to maybe have a small window that you work in, but it's always going to be some kind of adjustments.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.910
 And you want to adjust it a little bit more to develop that dish.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:30.910
 Sure.

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:33.040
 Then you maybe start to do a new dish.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.040
 And the thing is to just keep that same dish very similar.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:37.040
 That's hard.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:39.230
 Then you need to be really skilled.

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:40.540
 Right.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:44.580
 So you're fermenting tempeh from this Japanese process.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:46.580
 From the Indonesian.

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:48.800
 Indonesian process, yeah, excuse me.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:54.970
 And yeah, I just interviewed Sandor Katz, the fermentation guru of America.

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:56.970
 And yeah, he corrected me too.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:59.970
 The world, I guess, is kind of well-known outside.

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:02.180
 But wow.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:05.180
 So, but can you describe just how, well, where do you make it?

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:07.180
 How much are you making and where are you doing it?

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:14.180
 And this problem right now is I don't have anywhere to produce it, but I have customers.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:21.180
 So I have set up with a, with a proofer, like a normal bread proofer.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:22.170
 Sure.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:23.500
 Using that one.

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:30.070
 And because it's easy to work and creating a good room and atmosphere for the tempeh because

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:33.050
 it needs moist and it needs air.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:35.130
 So right now I'm doing it at home.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:43.690
 I can test spaces and sell just a few people, but I don't sell like in a larger scale because

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:45.690
 I don't have the permits and whatnot.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:51.070
 But the thing is I need to try to figure out how, where I can produce it and produce in

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:55.740
 a larger scale because I have, I have customers already.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:56.740
 That's been kind of the interesting.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:57.740
 Sure.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:01.740
 The people like mailing and asking when you're going to sell and waiting for it.

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:04.090
 And they're curious.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:07.730
 We've got to be, we've got some attention.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:08.730
 And so, yeah.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:09.690
 Yeah.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:10.620
 Cool.

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:14.680
 I guess about that is that I like that you attribute a lot of like what you're doing

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:18.380
 to punk and DIY culture and stuff.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:19.380
 I think about that a lot.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.380
 Chelsea and I both talk about that, about a lot of these projects.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:27.530
 We learned how to make things happen a lot through, you know, making a punk band work

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:30.530
 and making zines when we were younger.

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:31.550
 Yeah.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:39.040
 Was music and punk culture, did that come before your interest in cooking and food?

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:41.320
 Of sorts.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:44.320
 It's, I come from a cooking family.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:45.900
 Okay.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:53.780
 It's always been lots of food, but I was always thinking I would never work in a restaurant.

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:56.780
 I would never work with food because it's so heavy.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:13:01.810
 I see my mom work with food or my lots of relatives been working with food.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:05.640
 And I was like, oh shit, I'm not going to work with food.

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:06.640
 I'm going to do like more.

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.600
 I wanted to be a photographer.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:13.420
 So I was just going a different direction.

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:14.420
 And then I became vegan.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:15.440
 Okay.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:16.440
 And straight edge.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:24.260
 And through that, I really started to get the interest of food.

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:32.140
 And that made me, to become a vegan made me interested in food from my, made it my own.

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:34.170
 Not taking from my family.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:38.550
 It just, the veganism made me really interested in food.

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:41.900
 And then I started to connect back to my family.

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:49.110
 But the whole thing with straight edge and veganism was really important in a personal development

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:52.220
 as a foodie, I guess.

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:55.210
 So in sorts, it started to, in the same time.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:56.410
 Right.

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:57.400
 For me, at least.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:58.390
 And then I started to do music.

00:13:58.390 --> 00:14:03.330
 I always done more electronic music, but yeah, in the same kind of way, but.

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:04.580
 Yeah, cool.

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:06.580
 And your childhood was in Turkey.

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:07.550
 Is that right?

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:10.920
 No, in a small city of West Coast, Sweden.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:11.920
 Oh, in the small.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:12.920
 Okay.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:13.920
 But your family's Turkish.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:15.490
 Yeah.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:18.490
 That's the, that's the food environment they grew up in.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:20.030
 Yeah.

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:26.030
 There's this big palette of ingredients and flavors and processes that you draw in to make

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:26.960
 foods.

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:32.120
 And it kind of gets propelled into the idea of creating new ones.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:33.310
 Yeah.

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:36.290
 Now, are you still a vegan?

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:37.990
 No.

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:38.990
 No.

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:42.760
 But I think something happened some years back.

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:44.240
 Uh huh.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:49.810
 Because I eat lots of plants and I eat lots of plant based proteins and whatnot.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:50.830
 Right.

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:53.950
 And I eat meat as well, but I kind of like to eat them at the same time.

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:59.040
 And when I was going to restaurant, I was people, they, they have this menu and this

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:04.250
 like, you have this small notice that says vegetarian or vegan.

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:07.640
 And I was like, what the hell?

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:10.700
 I want to eat some delicious plant based food sometimes.

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:11.700
 Right.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:19.620
 And no one just do this awesome dish with whatever they could use like chickpeas and

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:24.750
 doing in a very interesting, new, creative, culinary way.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.350
 But people don't.

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:29.040
 And I was like, yeah, people don't.

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.870
 I can do it.

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:38.330
 So I started to do a lots of vegan, more than vegan cooking that's influenced in from my

00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:41.550
 kind of personal perspective of what I like.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:50.810
 So I just started to work a lot with vegan food because it's nice and easy when I do projects

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:56.020
 because everybody can eat vegan if they don't have attitude.

00:15:56.020 --> 00:16:01.970
 And, and, and I had lots of friends who are still vegetarians and my partner, she's vegan.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:08.630
 And it's, it's fun to, to just make delicious food for people who are not used to getting

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:09.630
 delicious food.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:10.720
 Right.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:11.720
 I do it.

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:13.740
 For me, it's not about your work.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.870
 Making a vegan dish is I'm making a dish.

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:17.850
 I want to eat.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:19.320
 Right.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:20.320
 And I crave.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:23.480
 Sometimes I, I have favorite things that are plants.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:28.570
 And I think lots of chefs or people cooking is like, they always have favorite things.

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:31.070
 That's like exclusively animals.

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:34.070
 It's like, they really want to say that like foie gras.

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:37.100
 And, and I think there's so much other stuff that's nice.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:43.060
 That's vegetables and not this expensive things as truffles or truffle.

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:49.180
 I can like truffles as well, but it's, there are other stuff that is so insanely delicious

00:16:49.180 --> 00:17:00.160
 that people forget because we have this idea of dining and meat being like, like the golden

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:02.190
 calf.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:03.190
 Uh huh.

00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:04.510
 Yeah.

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:12.080
 So we, we just aim for the animal protein, like the center of the culinary idea, I guess.

00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:13.080
 And I think it's boring.

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:21.210
 And I want, for me to work with vegan food has been more to just let loose of boundaries

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:25.210
 and just trying to figure out how to create my way of cooking.

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:34.470
 And for me, it's been very much about going down to really basics and using it's very much

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:35.530
 how I create music.

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:42.510
 It's like, it should be much of using all the basic tastes, all the frequencies, so to

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:43.570
 speak.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:44.720
 Uh huh.

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:46.720
 And you should have the umami, you should have the bitter and whatnot.

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:51.960
 And some things, you know, if you're drawn to more kind of bass driven music, it's going

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:57.210
 to be lots of bass or it's more acoustic is different kind of balance.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:59.300
 But you always need the balance.

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:03.320
 And for me, the texture is so important for all of us.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:05.320
 We eat lots with the texture.

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:07.380
 So I started to work with texture.

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:14.050
 So it started similar in the same time as I start to cook a lot of vegan food.

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:19.650
 And I was thinking of when I create a dish, it should be have this idea.

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:22.680
 It should be three different textures in some way.

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:27.630
 They can be different from each other or it can be similar to each other.

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:36.760
 You can have different kind of chewiness or you have a crispy and smooth and chewy things

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:37.760
 and whatnot.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:45.520
 But in the same way as doing, you know, culture as people did punk music or people did this

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:52.880
 this idea of creating whatever, when it comes to culture and art or, and I just took what

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:59.440
 I've been doing for the last 20 years and just trying to figure out to create that in, in

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:00.410
 food.

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:02.700
 I mean, yeah, that does make sense.

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:05.690
 And, um, it really, it strikes me a lot.

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:10.770
 How you talk about cooking the way, uh, you know, as an art practice.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:11.950
 Yeah.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:16.070
 So it's like you have one foot in cooking world and one foot in the art world, but the way that

00:19:16.070 --> 00:19:22.600
 you talk about it, it seems like very harmoniously the same thing, the same kind of practice for

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:23.600
 you.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:27.590
 I mean, I know that you see cooking as a creative practice.

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:31.590
 Is that something that you consciously chose or did that always just make sense to you?

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:34.600
 It's makes sense to me.

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:41.270
 And, and I, and I start to kind of, I was trying to figure out the way that was my cooking and

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:42.900
 my practice.

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:50.270
 So for me, it's been really good to use this artistic way and the science together.

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:57.800
 And I think when people say that chefs are like artists or, I never understood really what

00:19:57.800 --> 00:20:03.100
 I meant because I didn't see any artistness in the cook, in the food making.

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:09.350
 And so when, when people just do a plate or you should eat with your eyes and stuff.

00:20:09.350 --> 00:20:18.790
 And, and I just saw like expensive produce and I didn't see like creative plates being made.

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:23.360
 And they just copying each other in time to time.

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:25.700
 And they often pretty boring.

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:33.620
 There are, of course, there are people who has this really, their personal, no, not everyone

00:20:33.620 --> 00:20:37.770
 should have like their way, but, and I, I copy a lot.

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:47.180
 But I think there are more people who should just try to make a plate that they are theirs

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:55.770
 in, in, in like an aesthetic aspect more than it's the way people think that a plate should,

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:56.770
 should look like.

00:20:56.770 --> 00:21:03.740
 People just do how a plate should look like more than how they wanted it to be presented.

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:10.220
 I think there are, there are chefs and this really have this more art practice as well.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:17.530
 But I think that the cooking is very much of a craftsmanship and these old school people

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:27.670
 and people went to like more France, if a French cooking, they develop a very similar way

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:31.980
 on approaching that's more craftsmanship than artistic.

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:38.870
 And I had that before I went to university and, and just start to learn more stuff.

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:45.850
 And then I was more intrigued to work because I always been working artistically.

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:51.320
 And food is very much its culture and it's, it's a way to do things.

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:57.880
 So it's like, I can do a art, I can do a practice from the skills I have and it's artistic.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:02.120
 And I see very few people though, who work in that way.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:05.200
 I don't know if I make any sense here.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.200
 No, that, no, that's exactly what I meant.

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:08.200
 Yeah.

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:09.160
 Yeah, for sure.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:12.160
 Also I had, you call yourself a meal ecologist, which I love that.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:15.160
 I want to ask you what that means to you.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:16.160
 Exactly.

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:20.620
 It's in the university I went to.

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:31.190
 They made a new program some years back called Meal Ecology or they call it Culinary Arts and Ecology nowadays, I think, in English.

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:36.950
 But in Sweden it's Multinpsycholog, just badly translated to Meal Ecology.

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:43.970
 But it's, it's, it's, no matter what it is, but because in Sweden we created something called Meal Science.

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:49.000
 It's, in Swedish it's different, but because they talk about the craftsmanship a lot as well.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.090
 And they don't want to use the word science.

00:22:51.090 --> 00:23:02.240
 Then they started this program that they have, the half of the time you go to environmental science institution.

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:06.930
 And the other is you go to a restaurant college.

00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:15.430
 And so you learn like very natural science in one part, and then you learn the whole meal aspect in one part.

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:18.430
 So that's, that's basically what that education is.

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:37.700
 And to, to be a really generalist, to connect both what's going on in the soil to how, and what, what different crops have for different qualities in, in, in, in the way of, of growing them and eating them.

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:44.390
 And how they work when they, when they, when they are products, what you can do with them, how you can cook them.

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:51.400
 And how that are connected to each other, both like in the, in the stove and in the soil.

00:23:51.400 --> 00:24:10.200
 So we learn how all the way from that kind of small things, how they interconnect, but also the big scale in form of how, how we eat together, the whole, uh, social anthropology and, uh, environmental issues and so on.

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:17.190
 Right. No, I mean, I think I like that term meal ecology, cause, um, uh, I'm, I'm, I'm trained as an ecologist also.

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:27.380
 And, uh, there's this sense that an ecology that you can really closely observe the parts of an ecosystem and you can try to think functionally about how they all fit together.

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:35.370
 But there's also this, uh, aspect of it always knowing that it's not, you're never going to fully get what's happening.

00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:45.550
 You can kind of pull on the threads and you can theorize about the systems, but the complexity is so great that you always know there's some unpredictability in it.

00:24:45.550 --> 00:24:50.010
 That makes a lot, that's also a lot how it feels to cook something new.

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:57.120
 Yeah. And I think what you described is very much of the, the, the description of, uh, food culture.

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:06.310
 Um, it's very, it's a, it's a, it's an, it's a system and it's small habitats just interconnecting.

00:25:06.310 --> 00:25:15.820
 Again, you have this individuals who going from different habitats and it's pulling and interacting with each other as a ecosystem can do.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:26.080
 And some parts is not important to each other, but some are, and some doesn't need it, but in the whole scale, they need each other.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:27.020
 Right. Yeah.

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:35.190
 I guess a lot of what your work does, it puts into question or like it plays on this idea of traditional foods in some way.

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:45.350
 And, um, making something like a tempeh out of fava beans, it makes it harder to imagine that there's something we could purely call traditional food.

00:25:45.350 --> 00:25:56.760
 Cause as, as you pull back on that thread, you see that there's been innovation and change going on all the time and making fermented foods like tempeh, um, as they move across the world.

00:25:56.760 --> 00:25:57.860
 Yeah.

00:25:57.860 --> 00:26:00.860
 So, I mean, I know you think about that.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:10.210
 There's a lot of interest that seems like in traditional foods and I'm just intrigued by the way that you make sure that those aren't just, that's not just a simple trite thing.

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:11.870
 Yeah.

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:15.380
 So, uh, so what's the question?

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:24.380
 Have you ever thought like, man, I really want to get back to traditional foods and then felt like that leads somewhere more interesting than you originally thought?

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:29.600
 I think I'm kind of, I'm always, I'm always connected to the tradition.

00:26:29.600 --> 00:26:30.690
 Right.

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:35.570
 The thing is, I see it should go to jazz music.

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:53.710
 I think that's the best way to kind of explain what I'm doing because you had, you had these guys, Don Cherry and Onet Coleman coming from, from the South and they did this really insane jazz.

00:26:53.710 --> 00:27:02.150
 They were just free form, but when you listen to it, you hear the blues, you hear the tradition kind of well.

00:27:02.150 --> 00:27:04.650
 You hear where they come from.

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:11.220
 And, and Miles Davis and all those old jazz legends, they were come from kind of tradition.

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:12.600
 Okay.

00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:21.650
 He was from the middle of class, but, and then people were musicians, but anyways, music wise, they've learned the whole way.

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:27.670
 And you hear the, in some things you hear the blues a lot and some things you don't.

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:37.400
 But I think when it comes to my cooking, I'm, I'm rooted very much in like two traditions, like the Anatolian, the Turkish and the Swedish.

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:41.770
 But they started to have this more, more than like new Nordic.

00:27:41.770 --> 00:27:52.880
 And then in the new Nordic, they were really trying to connect back to the, to the produce we had in Europe, in the Northern Europe, rather than see what they had elsewhere.

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:57.540
 This was delicious, was more than do we have this delicious.

00:27:57.540 --> 00:28:18.940
 So I think my food is always, is based very much on tradition in some regards and lots of ideas I take from there, but I maybe apply them more scientifically sometimes, but it's without the tradition of without the kind of science and the more intellectual part of it and artistic way.

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:24.710
 I couldn't do the things I'm doing, but it's quite important, but traditional food.

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:26.710
 I don't know what it is actually.

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:29.640
 No, it's, it's what you said.

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:31.920
 Um, food is always developing.

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:33.010
 Right.

00:28:33.010 --> 00:28:46.560
 I think you take jazz, for instance, as a, if you listen to the early recordings from the twenties, it's very different from what happened in mid sixties, late sixties to the seventies.

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:47.700
 It's a long way.

00:28:47.700 --> 00:29:02.390
 And if you take it just one record from the twenties and one record from the seventies, it's hard to maybe understand they are really connected, but I think that's the development and that's the beauty of food, I guess.

00:29:02.390 --> 00:29:07.340
 Or the part I like is that it's always in a process.

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:27.840
 It's kind of important to understand your own tradition and knowledge in your own palette to kind of figure out the next cultures palette and what they have and try to figure out what's the thing that made theirs.

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:40.830
 I think if you understand your own traditional cooking is going to be so much easier to understand someone else cooking and you can approach them kind of easy.

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:42.940
 Yeah, no, that makes, that makes sense.

00:29:42.940 --> 00:29:43.940
 Sometimes it's hard.

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:49.070
 Sometimes you need to just try and try and then you maybe get some things wrong.

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:50.230
 Then you need to ask people.

00:29:50.230 --> 00:30:06.950
 But I think, and the thing is more I learned when I went to university and learned the things I learned in the academic world made me understand and approach personal and the tradition I come from in a better way.

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:14.280
 And then made me better observer and a creative to take things from others.

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:15.690
 Yeah.

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:17.230
 I hope so.

00:30:17.230 --> 00:30:18.230
 At least.

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:23.700
 Now you've got me thinking about what my tradition or food tradition would be there.

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:25.830
 What my palette that I come from would be.

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:29.780
 I'm trying to kind of, it's kind of a hard question, isn't it?

00:30:29.780 --> 00:30:30.780
 It's like a.

00:30:30.780 --> 00:30:31.810
 Yeah.

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:38.830
 I mean, for me, it's probably, I think it's influenced by hippie parents and a lot of experiments and back to the land.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:40.830
 My parents having a great garden and.

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:42.320
 Yeah.

00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:43.470
 But what is it?

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:44.470
 What is it for you?

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:48.450
 I know you've got a Turkish influence and a Swedish influence.

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:49.940
 Is it?

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:50.910
 Yeah.

00:30:50.910 --> 00:30:55.280
 Is it easy to describe your palette that you grew up with or that, that you come from?

00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:56.280
 Yeah.

00:30:56.280 --> 00:30:57.280
 In based on those two things.

00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:03.850
 And I had a mother who had lots of friends from different parts of the world from like Sri Lanka.

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:07.260
 And, and I had a mother who was really open to the world.

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:08.260
 Right.

00:31:08.260 --> 00:31:13.820
 So I come from this, that you should always try once.

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:16.740
 Then you can say what you think about it.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:20.250
 You can't say anything before you taste something.

00:31:20.250 --> 00:31:25.660
 So you, I've learned because then I started just try things.

00:31:25.660 --> 00:31:27.780
 And that made me more curious.

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:29.780
 I started to just eat and eat since I was a kid.

00:31:29.780 --> 00:31:30.780
 Yeah.

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:35.310
 That's a really important part of my palette.

00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:36.280
 I guess.

00:31:36.280 --> 00:31:41.560
 I think like an ethnic perspective, it's like Turkish and Swedish and whatnot.

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:52.630
 But I think the approach, like the technique my mother learned, learned me how to be open to the world, I guess, was equally important.

00:31:52.630 --> 00:32:05.500
 And having a lots of friends from like all over the world and bunch of people who didn't eat things like have vegetarian friends and so on.

00:32:05.500 --> 00:32:08.100
 I mean, it's very kind of mixed.

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:13.410
 I guess one other thing I'm curious about is speaking of being open to the world.

00:32:13.410 --> 00:32:26.830
 I know Sweden, there's this value of cosmopolitanism, but also this tension with a right wing that's an anti-immigrant, just like we have here in the United States right now.

00:32:26.830 --> 00:32:27.770
 Yeah.

00:32:27.770 --> 00:32:41.100
 Does that play out in food culture or do you interact with that tension between like the culture that values a big world and the right wing pushback on that?

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:43.100
 Does that play out in your practice as a chef?

00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:46.130
 For me, sort of, not so much.

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:51.120
 I think I've been more kind of, not reckless, but more don't give a shit.

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:56.150
 And because I'm really like the mix and my life is mixed with other people's culture.

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:57.430
 So I've been more eager to mix.

00:32:57.430 --> 00:33:10.430
 I've been more eager to be very clear that my food is inspired from my neighbors rather than cool chefs in New York.

00:33:10.430 --> 00:33:21.530
 And as I get inspired by living on the subway and hearing language, I have no idea where they're from and being so curious.

00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:24.980
 And I think that's kind of fascinating here.

00:33:24.980 --> 00:33:44.310
 You see these people, they're looking, you know, Chinese, Korean, but they talk some kind of Russian and, you know, and you start to see, I have, it's like 160 nationalities in my neighborhood and that stuff.

00:33:44.310 --> 00:33:50.660
 And the tension here, I start to think about it more and more and in my cooking as well.

00:33:50.660 --> 00:34:00.080
 And the thing is, I use a lot of Swedish produce and they are very much like, oh, we should be eating like Swedish food.

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:02.080
 And they have no idea what Swedish food is.

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:07.070
 And for me has been, I've started to learn more about Swedish cooking as well.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:11.030
 And I like some things that's like super typical Swedish.

00:34:11.030 --> 00:34:14.030
 And from the north, we have a fermented fish.

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:19.450
 That's like, if you translate, it's like sour herring, but it's really heavy.

00:34:19.450 --> 00:34:20.450
 And then people in the north eat it.

00:34:20.450 --> 00:34:31.650
 And when I started to eat it, I was vegan back then, but it was like the thing that made me turn away from veganism, I guess, because I really like the fermented food.

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:41.590
 Now, if nowadays, maybe I wouldn't turn away from veganism in that regard, because now I learned there's so much other stuff that's fermented than vegetables.

00:34:41.590 --> 00:34:48.950
 And the thing is, I approach that cooking because it's a long Swedish tradition, but I love it so much.

00:34:48.950 --> 00:34:59.870
 And I think as a Swede, I think I can, if I, this is the tricky part, because I eat my, this fish in a different way that they do.

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:13.340
 I eat with a different kind of bread, more Indian sort of chapati bread, but then I use chili sauce, because the fermented food I like is often very strong.

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:16.300
 And chili is like kimchi and very Korean.

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:21.870
 So I was thinking that why can't I just do this fish in a way I like.

00:35:21.870 --> 00:35:34.570
 And I think often when people with these tensions and the right wing, they, they just, they don't want to change or get influenced with the food they have here.

00:35:34.570 --> 00:35:38.920
 And I think influences and dishes is just constantly changing.

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:40.920
 We have a dolma here in Sweden.

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:47.090
 People say the various Swedish traditional, but it came with Turkish bankiers in the 18th century.

00:35:47.090 --> 00:35:53.150
 So for me, it's been very much working with this, that culture and food.

00:35:53.150 --> 00:35:59.520
 Certainly food is very much about being influenced and always in a constant mood.

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:04.900
 You're always, wherever you see or go, it always rubs you off something.

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:16.010
 And that's makes people maybe making this radical changes when they cook, when they've been somewhere, there's always small kind of changes.

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:20.970
 And I think when I work, it's, I'm trying to just highlight that sometimes more and more.

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:25.970
 And I'm going to have a small workshop this summer and talk about the new cuisine.

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:36.130
 And I think when you are a person who comes, you have parents from somewhere and you grew up in other places and like in Sweden and you're really connected to both.

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:46.560
 I think just that makes people kind of confused and, and you need to, the right wings, they don't like when it's mixed and when you're proud of both.

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:47.980
 Right.

00:36:47.980 --> 00:36:56.990
 And the dilemma here, like in a bigger sense, when you see cooking, they see everything gets so stereotypical.

00:36:56.990 --> 00:37:02.400
 You know, when they talk about food from Thailand, it's a Thai or it's whatever.

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:10.610
 And they talk about when it's food from Vietnam, it's just like spring rolls and so on.

00:37:10.610 --> 00:37:16.110
 And it's like, you'd never dig, you never dig deeper.

00:37:16.110 --> 00:37:20.030
 And I think that's very much here in Sweden.

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:22.700
 People just, it's very divided, very separate.

00:37:22.700 --> 00:37:28.790
 And they have this small interaction, but then it's very adjusted to the other people.

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:31.820
 And it's very, no, no kind of interactions.

00:37:31.820 --> 00:37:34.940
 And that's a huge dilemma as I see it.

00:37:34.940 --> 00:37:39.000
 And you don't see people from whatever China be.

00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:46.550
 There are some few people, the Chinese people who are skilled chefs and kind of proud of their culture and doing their thing here.

00:37:46.550 --> 00:37:55.930
 But often people just adjust themselves really hard to the Swedish society and just make something they think that people here like.

00:37:55.930 --> 00:37:57.920
 I think that's a part of that too.

00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:04.040
 And one right wing politician said this thing about kebab.

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:10.710
 This guy, they were talking about some foods is where it comes from and influences and things.

00:38:10.710 --> 00:38:15.140
 And he said like, yeah, but the kebab here, it's Swedish.

00:38:15.140 --> 00:38:18.570
 And they were like, oh, yeah, it is.

00:38:18.570 --> 00:38:21.520
 So this other guy says, oh, you mean, so he's assimilated.

00:38:21.520 --> 00:38:24.560
 And the kebab we have here is very assimilated.

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:27.560
 It's very adjusted to the society here.

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:29.580
 And often food do that.

00:38:29.580 --> 00:38:31.670
 I think that's going to be problematic.

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:32.730
 Right.

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:34.720
 Of course, it's always adjusting.

00:38:34.720 --> 00:38:39.030
 But in whose terms do the food adjust?

00:38:39.030 --> 00:38:40.130
 Yeah.

00:38:40.130 --> 00:38:47.130
 And I also hope that some of the food is also a secret way into a lot of to the like xenophobic psyche sometimes.

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:52.410
 Do you think it is or do you do you hope it is?

00:38:52.410 --> 00:38:53.990
 Yeah, I think some.

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:56.010
 It doesn't always work out that way.

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:57.010
 Right.

00:38:57.010 --> 00:38:58.010
 For sure.

00:38:58.010 --> 00:39:00.550
 But no, but sometimes it is.

00:39:00.550 --> 00:39:02.610
